wrote:

>
>
>
> On Thu, May 15, 2014 at 3:33 PM, Ian Johnson <blindbravado@gmail.com>wrot=
e:
>
>> On Thu, May 15, 2014 at 11:58 AM, 'Wuzzy' via lojban <
>> lojban@googlegroups.com> wrote:
>>
>>> Of course, the question how to say this in Lojban came up.
>>> Xorxes suggested =E2=80=9Cbu letteral=E2=80=9D to be lojbanized as =E2=
=80=9Czo bu zei lerfu=E2=80=9D.
>>> This looks strange and unintitive to me. I am not even sure if this is
>>> grammatical and if yes, under _which_ grammar? xD PEG? YACC? Something
>>> else?
>>>
>> After the Magic Words reform, which to my understanding coincided with
>> the writing of the PEG, {zo WORD} is a single word for purposes of word
>> counting.
>>
>
> Sort of.  It's a single word for those magic words that look back (so for
> "bu", for the left side of "zei", for "si"), but it's not a single word f=
or
> magic words that look forward (you can't quote it with "zo", you can't us=
e
> it as the right side component of "zei". Magic words that look forward wi=
ll
> just see "zo" as a word, so "zo zo cmavo" is a full bridi, not a quote of
> "zo cmavo".
>
> "zo bu zei lerfu" works both for PEG and for YACC, with the caveat that
> YACC uses a pre-parsing lexing process before the actual YACC kicks in.
>
>
>
>> Word counting is relevant to {si}, {bu}, and {zei}, and I think nothing
>> else, provided we exclude experimental cmavo. This means {zo si si} is t=
he
>> empty string, {zo bu zei lerfu} is a valid lujvo, and {zo bu bu} is a va=
lid
>> lerfu.
>>
>
> Yes, "si", "bu" and "zei" need to know what counts as "previous word". bu=
t
> "zo" and "zei" need to know what counts as "next word".
>
> mu'o mi'e xorxes
>
You're right, this is a better way of thinking about it. The way I was
thinking about it is inherently left-to-right: zu'u in {zo si si}, {zo si}
becomes a single word, then {si} sees it and deletes it. zu'unai in {ko'a
zei zo}, {zo} never gets a chance to quote something to form a single word
because {zei} eats it first.

mi'e la latro'a mu'o

